# Copy to docker/.env and edit.

# What the container runs:
#
#   web       the application's own web interface, and no window at all
#   both      that, and the window streamed to a browser as well
#   desktop   the streamed window only; the web interface and API are off
RB_MODE=both

# A user name and password for the streamed window (both and desktop modes).
# Left empty the stream is open to anyone who can reach its port. The web
# interface is separate: it asks for the token the container prints when it
# starts, and for the administrator password once one has been set.
RB_STREAM_USER=
RB_STREAM_PASSWORD=

# Which rclone to run, chosen when the container starts. Leave both empty to
# use the one in the image. Set either and it is fetched once and kept in the
# /config volume; changing it and restarting is all a change of version takes.
#
#   RB_RCLONE_REPO      tgdrive/rclone (has the teldrive backend) or rclone/rclone
#   RB_RCLONE_VERSION   1.75.0, or "latest" to ask GitHub at each start
#
# Not RCLONE_REPO / RCLONE_VERSION: rclone reads RCLONE_<FLAG> as that flag,
# and RCLONE_VERSION in a container breaks every rclone command in it.
#RB_RCLONE_REPO=rclone/rclone
#RB_RCLONE_VERSION=1.75.0

# Where rclone.conf, saved tasks and per-job logs live. Leave unset to use a
# Docker-managed volume, which works the same on Windows and Linux. Set it to
# a directory to keep the data on the host instead -- for example to carry on
# using an rclone.conf you already have:
#
#   RB_CONFIG=/home/you/.config/rclone
#   RB_CONFIG=C:\Users\you\AppData\Roaming\rclone
#RB_CONFIG=

# What the container can see to transfer. Unset means an empty managed volume.
#   RB_MEDIA=/mnt
#   RB_MEDIA=D:\Media
#RB_MEDIA=

# An rclone.conf kept elsewhere: uncomment the /rclone-config mount and the
# RCLONE_CONFIG line in compose.yaml. Mount the directory, not the file --
# see the comment there for why.

# Use an rclone from the host instead of the one in the image. Uncomment the
# matching line in compose.yaml as well. It must be a Linux binary for the
# container's architecture: a Windows rclone.exe cannot run inside a Linux
# container, so on Windows this means pointing at a Linux build.
#RB_RCLONE=/usr/local/bin/rclone

# Owner of the files written into a host directory. Ignored when using the
# managed volumes above. These were USER_ID and GROUP_ID before the base image
# changed.
#PUID=1000
#PGID=1000

# Host ports: the web interface, and the streamed window. Windows reserves
# scattered ranges for Hyper-V and refuses to bind inside them; the error
# talks about socket access permissions rather than the port being reserved.
# Check with:
#   netsh interface ipv4 show excludedportrange protocol=tcp
#RB_WEB_PORT=19999
#RB_PORT=3001

#TZ=Asia/Bangkok

# ---- compose.dev.yaml only --------------------------------------------------
# The development container has its own name, ports and volumes, so it can run
# beside the real one. Its settings are named RB_DEV_* for the same reason.

# web, both or desktop. Falls back to RB_MODE above.
#RB_DEV_MODE=web

#RB_DEV_WEB_PORT=19991
#RB_DEV_PORT=3011

# The rclone.conf to use. Defaults to this machine's own, mounted read-write:
# what changes a remote in the container changes it outside too. Point this at
# a directory holding a copy to keep the two apart.
#RB_DEV_RCLONE_CONF=D:/rclone-copy

# A directory holding a file called "rclone" to use instead of the one the
# image was built with. It has to be a Linux binary for the container's
# architecture -- the rclone.exe installed on Windows cannot run in there.
#RB_DEV_RCLONE_DIR=D:/rclone-linux

# Which rclone to run, chosen at start: change and restart, no rebuild.
#RB_DEV_RCLONE_REPO=rclone/rclone
#RB_DEV_RCLONE_VERSION=1.75.0

#RB_DEV_MEDIA=D:/Media
